1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,440
Disaster strikes.

2
00:00:04,540 --> 00:00:08,000
Human lives in imminent danger.

3
00:00:08,100 --> 00:00:12,940
Sophisticated tools can mean salvation.

4
00:00:13,040 --> 00:00:17,920
Now, rescue equipment on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,000
The End

6
00:00:45,420 --> 00:00:47,980
Victims trapped by swift water,

7
00:00:50,080 --> 00:00:53,020
caught unaware in a burning building,

8
00:00:54,960 --> 00:00:58,520
buried under rubble from explosions or earthquakes,

9
00:00:58,521 --> 00:01:00,860
Every day in all corners of the world,

10
00:01:00,960 --> 00:01:04,260
unforeseen disasters occur, from Oklahoma City

11
00:01:04,360 --> 00:01:06,520
to Los Angeles, California.

12
00:01:06,620 --> 00:01:09,860
When a highly trained, highly technical rescue response

13
00:01:09,960 --> 00:01:15,680
is required, the call goes to the USAR team.

14
00:01:15,780 --> 00:01:19,280
The acronym USAR stands for Urban Search and Rescue.

15
00:01:19,380 --> 00:01:24,580
And basically, it is a technical side of rescue work.

16
00:01:24,581 --> 00:01:28,780
We deal with things like structure collapse.

17
00:01:28,880 --> 00:01:32,420
We do technical rope work where we can go down and get people.

18
00:01:32,520 --> 00:01:35,820
Also in the wildland environment, up in the canyons,

19
00:01:35,920 --> 00:01:40,260
if a car goes over the side, we perform that type of rescue.

20
00:01:40,360 --> 00:01:43,700
Also vehicular type incidents on the freeway,

21
00:01:43,800 --> 00:01:49,800
pretty broad category of things that we get involved in.

22
00:01:49,900 --> 00:01:52,000
The fire department in Los Angeles

23
00:01:52,001 --> 00:01:56,100
formed their special USAR units in 1985.

24
00:01:56,200 --> 00:01:59,240
They are trained for technical, high-risk missions

25
00:01:59,340 --> 00:02:01,940
and are proficient in using the most up-to-date rescue

26
00:02:02,040 --> 00:02:03,340
technology.

27
00:02:03,440 --> 00:02:05,580
But some of the basic tools they employ

28
00:02:05,680 --> 00:02:08,980
date back over 50 years.

29
00:02:09,080 --> 00:02:11,420
I know that the discipline of urban search and rescue

30
00:02:11,520 --> 00:02:13,760
has probably been around at least since the Second World

31
00:02:13,860 --> 00:02:17,100
War and probably had its origins over in Europe,

32
00:02:17,200 --> 00:02:18,860
where you had the constant bombings.

33
00:02:18,960 --> 00:02:21,300
They had to deal with collapsed structures

34
00:02:21,301 --> 00:02:23,340
on a constant basis.

35
00:02:23,440 --> 00:02:25,780
During the London Blitz in England,

36
00:02:25,880 --> 00:02:28,480
civil defense engineers devised plans

37
00:02:28,580 --> 00:02:30,940
for stabilizing buildings that collapsed

38
00:02:31,040 --> 00:02:33,380
under the barrage of bombings.

39
00:02:33,480 --> 00:02:37,220
The tools they employed, picks, shovels, crowbars, chains,

40
00:02:37,320 --> 00:02:40,820
and gin poles, were used to move the debris away

41
00:02:40,920 --> 00:02:43,980
and enable the rescuers to search for victims buried

42
00:02:44,080 --> 00:02:45,020
beneath the rubble.

43
00:02:45,021 --> 00:02:51,500
Today, the USAR team still implement a shoring technique

44
00:02:51,600 --> 00:02:53,200
from World War II.

45
00:02:53,300 --> 00:02:55,360
They use plywood planks and struts

46
00:02:55,460 --> 00:02:58,360
to secure the collapsed or caved-in walls of buildings

47
00:02:58,460 --> 00:03:00,840
or earthen trenches.

48
00:03:00,940 --> 00:03:04,800
So the shoring is put in place primarily for the rescuer

49
00:03:04,900 --> 00:03:08,380
because we don't want to create another victim.

50
00:03:08,480 --> 00:03:09,980
And then secondary, obviously, is

51
00:03:10,080 --> 00:03:13,840
to get the victim out of the hole in a safe manner.

52
00:03:13,841 --> 00:03:18,440
Shoring is just one step in any collapsed structure procedure.

53
00:03:18,540 --> 00:03:20,640
If the victim has fallen into a small hole

54
00:03:20,740 --> 00:03:22,980
or down a narrow shaft, the rescuers

55
00:03:23,080 --> 00:03:26,780
have to find a way to get to them and bring them up to safety.

56
00:03:26,880 --> 00:03:31,500
These are called confined space rescues.

57
00:03:31,600 --> 00:03:36,400
In 1949, four-year-old Kathy Fiscus fell into an abandoned

58
00:03:36,500 --> 00:03:39,500
oil well in San Marino, California, near Los Angeles.

59
00:03:39,501 --> 00:03:42,840
Los Angeles.

60
00:03:42,940 --> 00:03:45,700
Television cameras broadcast the rescue efforts,

61
00:03:45,800 --> 00:03:50,540
marking the first rescue attempt to appear live on the airwaves.

62
00:03:50,640 --> 00:03:52,940
The nation watched for 27 and 1 1

63
00:03:53,040 --> 00:03:56,860
hours as the rescuers worked to get Kathy out of the tiny, deep

64
00:03:56,960 --> 00:03:57,960
hole.

65
00:03:58,060 --> 00:04:01,580
The rescuers toiled for two days as the country held its

66
00:04:01,680 --> 00:04:03,260
collective breath.

67
00:04:03,360 --> 00:04:05,760
But when they finally got down to where she was,

68
00:04:05,860 --> 00:04:07,600
it was too late.

69
00:04:07,601 --> 00:04:11,440
The rescuers just didn't have the tools necessary to extricate

70
00:04:11,540 --> 00:04:14,640
Kathy from the well in time.

71
00:04:14,740 --> 00:04:16,340
It appeared in the footage that I saw

72
00:04:16,440 --> 00:04:19,540
that they just had a large clamshell excavator.

73
00:04:19,640 --> 00:04:23,240
And they were just digging large quantities of dirt

74
00:04:23,340 --> 00:04:27,160
out from the area where she was entrapped in the well.

75
00:04:27,260 --> 00:04:28,760
That's very time consuming.

76
00:04:28,860 --> 00:04:31,460
The very well that Kathy Fiskis was trapped in,

77
00:04:31,560 --> 00:04:33,860
we can get that type of company to assist us

78
00:04:33,960 --> 00:04:37,400
by drilling a hole large enough to put a rescuer in right next

79
00:04:37,401 --> 00:04:40,800
and then we can just lower a rescuer down into that hole,

80
00:04:40,900 --> 00:04:43,240
and then they can breach through to where the victim is

81
00:04:43,340 --> 00:04:45,000
and bring them out.

82
00:04:45,100 --> 00:04:48,940
Rescuers now have a new device called the Search Cam

83
00:04:49,040 --> 00:04:51,440
that assists them in locating victims trapped

84
00:04:51,540 --> 00:04:54,460
in confined spaces.

85
00:04:54,560 --> 00:04:57,460
The video camera is mounted on a long probe.

86
00:04:57,560 --> 00:05:00,060
It can be inserted into a hole and rotated

87
00:05:00,160 --> 00:05:02,060
to get the maximum visibility.

88
00:05:02,160 --> 00:05:05,500
There is a microphone built into the camera and a speaker,

89
00:05:05,501 --> 00:05:08,500
speaker, so if there is a victim that is covered by debris

90
00:05:08,600 --> 00:05:13,100
in a hole, he or she can respond to the rescuer's calls.

91
00:05:13,200 --> 00:05:15,440
The search cam is a high tech tool

92
00:05:15,540 --> 00:05:18,540
that enables rescuers to get in places that were previously

93
00:05:18,640 --> 00:05:20,540
inaccessible.

94
00:05:20,640 --> 00:05:23,360
The search cam was first used in 1993

95
00:05:23,460 --> 00:05:25,760
to assist the New York City firefighters

96
00:05:25,860 --> 00:05:29,220
in the aftermath of the World Trade Center bombing.

97
00:05:29,320 --> 00:05:32,060
It's been used to uncover victims in the Oklahoma City

98
00:05:32,061 --> 00:05:36,760
bombing and the bombing of the embassies in Africa.

99
00:05:36,860 --> 00:05:39,100
Prior to having those devices, we just

100
00:05:39,200 --> 00:05:41,000
had to go into every collapsed building

101
00:05:41,100 --> 00:05:43,680
and dig through the rubble to check for people.

102
00:05:43,780 --> 00:05:46,840
And now we can search these buildings

103
00:05:46,940 --> 00:05:51,380
with a little less of an all out assault on the building.

104
00:05:51,480 --> 00:05:54,480
We can check the building, see if there are any survivors.

105
00:05:54,580 --> 00:05:56,880
And then if there aren't, we can move on to where

106
00:05:56,980 --> 00:06:01,520
we can use our resources in a more prudent manner.

107
00:06:01,521 --> 00:06:04,860
In the case of fires, the rescue units employ a new device

108
00:06:04,960 --> 00:06:07,720
called the thermal imaging camera,

109
00:06:07,820 --> 00:06:11,300
which differentiates shapes in a smoke-filled room.

110
00:06:11,400 --> 00:06:13,540
Up until now, the firefighters have simply

111
00:06:13,640 --> 00:06:15,200
had to crawl through the structure

112
00:06:15,300 --> 00:06:16,740
and feel for any victims.

113
00:06:19,900 --> 00:06:23,100
The USAR teams are also called in to conduct swift water

114
00:06:23,200 --> 00:06:24,040
rescues.

115
00:06:24,140 --> 00:06:26,820
The rescue units hold water safety seminars

116
00:06:26,920 --> 00:06:28,680
to educate the public.

117
00:06:28,681 --> 00:06:32,680
but they still have to be prepared for any circumstance.

118
00:06:32,780 --> 00:06:36,080
We try to initially start off with public awareness,

119
00:06:36,180 --> 00:06:38,820
which would basically eliminate our job.

120
00:06:38,920 --> 00:06:41,320
But if it doesn't work, we start.

121
00:06:41,420 --> 00:06:43,820
Our next step is what they call land base.

122
00:06:43,920 --> 00:06:46,900
We try to rescue the person from land, throw them a rope,

123
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:48,640
throw them a buoy, whatever.

124
00:06:48,740 --> 00:06:51,740
If that fails, if the person's been in the water too long

125
00:06:51,840 --> 00:06:54,280
and they're not as strong enough to hang on to the rope,

126
00:06:54,380 --> 00:06:56,840
we can do a contact rescue.

127
00:06:56,841 --> 00:07:00,280
A contact rescue entails lowering a rescuer on a rope

128
00:07:00,380 --> 00:07:02,280
to where the victim is.

129
00:07:02,380 --> 00:07:05,420
Once contact has been made, the rescuer hooks himself

130
00:07:05,520 --> 00:07:09,660
to the victim, and the two are hoisted back to safe ground.

131
00:07:09,760 --> 00:07:12,860
This is a method that dates back to the Second World War.

132
00:07:17,300 --> 00:07:22,600
There is even a special lifting device for large animals.

133
00:07:22,700 --> 00:07:26,300
The animal control people have developed a harness

134
00:07:26,301 --> 00:07:28,260
specifically for horses.

135
00:07:28,360 --> 00:07:31,380
It's made out of steel with nylon webbing,

136
00:07:31,480 --> 00:07:33,040
and we can attach it to a horse.

137
00:07:33,140 --> 00:07:35,040
And we've seen it in the flood control channels,

138
00:07:35,140 --> 00:07:36,980
where they get caught in mud and rock.

139
00:07:37,080 --> 00:07:38,940
And we'll attach this harness to them.

140
00:07:39,040 --> 00:07:42,480
In that case, out in the open, we'll use a helicopter hoist,

141
00:07:42,580 --> 00:07:45,120
come in, hover over the horse, clip into the harness,

142
00:07:45,220 --> 00:07:46,620
and then lift the horse right out.

143
00:07:50,860 --> 00:07:53,420
Once the victims are safe, the next phase

144
00:07:53,520 --> 00:07:55,500
of the rescue takes place.

145
00:07:55,501 --> 00:07:57,840
On-scene medical care.

146
00:07:57,841 --> 00:08:00,940
Alan Cowan is the retired chief of paramedics

147
00:08:00,941 --> 00:08:02,940
for the LA Fire Department.

148
00:08:02,941 --> 00:08:06,340
He was on the force for 32 years.

149
00:08:06,341 --> 00:08:09,340
The tools of the trade when I began my career

150
00:08:09,341 --> 00:08:11,920
in emergency medicine, and I hesitate to call it that,

151
00:08:11,921 --> 00:08:13,460
because really, back in those days,

152
00:08:13,461 --> 00:08:16,380
it was a van with a gurney and a stretcher,

153
00:08:16,381 --> 00:08:21,160
a tank of oxygen, a first aid kit, some rubber splints.

154
00:08:21,161 --> 00:08:22,220
That was it.

155
00:08:22,221 --> 00:08:24,960
In fact, many ambulance companies worked out

156
00:08:24,961 --> 00:08:29,760
of mortuaries to show you the mentality.

157
00:08:29,860 --> 00:08:32,460
Alan learned rescue medicine by being on the job

158
00:08:32,560 --> 00:08:34,800
with seasoned professionals.

159
00:08:34,900 --> 00:08:36,700
They didn't do things as well as today.

160
00:08:36,800 --> 00:08:39,340
They didn't know how to put cervical collars on,

161
00:08:39,440 --> 00:08:42,120
and they didn't know how to put backboards and binders

162
00:08:42,220 --> 00:08:44,040
on patients and getting them packaged

163
00:08:44,140 --> 00:08:45,520
ready for transportation.

164
00:08:45,620 --> 00:08:48,120
But they sure knew a lot more about bandaging

165
00:08:48,220 --> 00:08:50,380
than most people do today.

166
00:08:50,480 --> 00:08:53,360
Alan remembers his first rescue attempt,

167
00:08:53,361 --> 00:08:57,660
trying to save a young girl who had been hit by a car.

168
00:08:57,760 --> 00:08:59,940
And I remember I had read about putting your mouth

169
00:09:00,040 --> 00:09:03,200
over somebody's mouth and blowing the breath of life.

170
00:09:03,300 --> 00:09:04,400
I had read about it in a book.

171
00:09:04,500 --> 00:09:08,640
It wasn't being done in 1963 to any great extent.

172
00:09:08,740 --> 00:09:10,680
So I put my mouth on hers.

173
00:09:10,780 --> 00:09:13,240
I lifted the back of her head up.

174
00:09:13,340 --> 00:09:14,780
And I blew the air in.

175
00:09:14,880 --> 00:09:16,620
And I remember in the back of the ambulance,

176
00:09:16,720 --> 00:09:19,420
as I could hear the siren going, she looked up at me.

177
00:09:19,520 --> 00:09:20,720
And I remember her eyes.

178
00:09:23,360 --> 00:09:26,540
They still remember it today.

179
00:09:26,541 --> 00:09:28,660
With any on-site response team,

180
00:09:28,661 --> 00:09:31,740
time is always of the essence.

181
00:09:31,741 --> 00:09:34,880
Stabilizing the victim and getting them to a hospital

182
00:09:34,881 --> 00:09:37,980
is the first priority.

183
00:09:37,981 --> 00:09:40,420
The only difference today is it's very organized.

184
00:09:40,421 --> 00:09:41,780
We do the same things.

185
00:09:41,781 --> 00:09:46,180
We triage, we treat, and we transport.

186
00:09:46,181 --> 00:09:49,420
Triage is to sort out those individuals that are critically

187
00:09:49,421 --> 00:09:50,860
hurt from those that are not.

188
00:09:50,861 --> 00:09:53,600
We treat, we try to stabilize a patient,

189
00:09:53,601 --> 00:09:54,600
and then we transport.

190
00:09:54,601 --> 00:09:59,060
And that's what we did 30 and 40 and 50 years ago.

191
00:09:59,061 --> 00:10:02,100
For paramedics, the most significant technological

192
00:10:02,101 --> 00:10:06,180
advance in the last 10 years is the portable defibrillator

193
00:10:06,181 --> 00:10:09,380
for heart attack patients.

194
00:10:09,381 --> 00:10:12,340
In this video, you can see a man having a heart attack

195
00:10:12,341 --> 00:10:14,180
in a casino.

196
00:10:14,181 --> 00:10:16,460
Because the casino security personnel

197
00:10:16,461 --> 00:10:18,820
had a portable defibrillator, they

198
00:10:18,821 --> 00:10:22,360
were able to save the man's life.

199
00:10:22,361 --> 00:10:24,120
It reads out your EKG.

200
00:10:24,121 --> 00:10:25,460
It tells you when to shock.

201
00:10:25,461 --> 00:10:26,620
It talks to you.

202
00:10:26,621 --> 00:10:28,220
It says, get your hands off.

203
00:10:28,221 --> 00:10:28,960
Shock now.

204
00:10:28,961 --> 00:10:30,220
Shock advised.

205
00:10:30,221 --> 00:10:33,280
But I think there'll come a time where we'll have a mobile

206
00:10:33,281 --> 00:10:36,260
operating room that goes out to the patient, rather than bring

207
00:10:36,261 --> 00:10:38,420
the patient to the hospital.

208
00:10:38,421 --> 00:10:43,020
There are exciting times coming in emergency medicine.

209
00:10:43,021 --> 00:10:43,980
Hope I get to see them.

210
00:10:48,820 --> 00:10:52,820
For over 200 years, the United States Coast Guard

211
00:10:52,920 --> 00:10:54,900
has been providing rescue services

212
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:57,800
to mariners in distress.

213
00:10:57,900 --> 00:11:00,340
Well, the Coast Guard has an overall strength

214
00:11:00,440 --> 00:11:04,640
of about 36,000 to 37,000 people.

215
00:11:04,740 --> 00:11:07,040
And actually, everyone in the Coast Guard,

216
00:11:07,140 --> 00:11:09,880
in some way or another, supports the search and rescue mission.

217
00:11:09,980 --> 00:11:13,640
Every year, the Coast Guard saves approximately 5,000 lives.

218
00:11:13,641 --> 00:11:19,440
We respond to about 65,000 search and rescue calls.

219
00:11:19,540 --> 00:11:24,020
In addition, we also facilitate, through our various

220
00:11:24,120 --> 00:11:28,420
communication systems, responses by other countries,

221
00:11:28,520 --> 00:11:32,620
either in the Pacific or the Caribbean and so forth.

222
00:11:32,720 --> 00:11:34,920
This mighty seafaring force has

223
00:11:35,020 --> 00:11:39,600
been evolving since 1790, when Alexander Hamilton established

224
00:11:39,700 --> 00:11:41,760
the Revenue Cutter Service, which

225
00:11:41,761 --> 00:11:44,760
which was basically a tax collection agency for goods

226
00:11:44,860 --> 00:11:50,000
landing in the United States on ships from foreign countries.

227
00:11:50,100 --> 00:11:53,500
So they had to have a armed presence

228
00:11:53,600 --> 00:11:56,500
or a group of people that enforced those taxes.

229
00:11:56,600 --> 00:11:59,840
And so they established 10 cutters initially

230
00:11:59,940 --> 00:12:03,840
to patrol the different major coasts of the United States.

231
00:12:03,940 --> 00:12:07,760
Foreign immigration and trade grew along with the nation,

232
00:12:07,860 --> 00:12:09,880
and therefore so did the number of shipwrecks

233
00:12:09,881 --> 00:12:12,820
that occurred on American shores.

234
00:12:12,920 --> 00:12:16,220
In 1848, the federal government began organizing

235
00:12:16,320 --> 00:12:19,720
official rescue sheds along the eastern seaboard.

236
00:12:19,820 --> 00:12:22,920
These sheds were equipped with the rescue equipment of the day,

237
00:12:23,020 --> 00:12:25,660
a few boats and an apparatus to send a line out

238
00:12:25,760 --> 00:12:27,440
to the vessel in distress.

239
00:12:27,540 --> 00:12:31,040
The equipment was crude and not always safe.

240
00:12:31,140 --> 00:12:33,740
And the biggest problem was finding trained individuals

241
00:12:33,840 --> 00:12:36,040
who could oversee the rescue.

242
00:12:36,140 --> 00:12:38,880
Sumner Increase Kimball addressed that problem

243
00:12:38,881 --> 00:12:41,280
problem in 1871.

244
00:12:41,380 --> 00:12:43,720
Kimball was the head of the revenue cutter service

245
00:12:43,820 --> 00:12:46,420
at the time, and he began manning the shed,

246
00:12:46,520 --> 00:12:48,660
sometimes with a team of rescuers,

247
00:12:48,760 --> 00:12:51,360
and sometimes with just one station keeper

248
00:12:51,460 --> 00:12:54,300
to maintain the equipment and organize the rescue teams

249
00:12:54,400 --> 00:12:55,760
when needed.

250
00:12:55,860 --> 00:12:58,960
By 1878, Kimball established a separate bureau

251
00:12:59,060 --> 00:13:01,940
within the government, appropriately called the United

252
00:13:02,040 --> 00:13:05,900
States Life-Saving Service.

253
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:08,680
I can't imagine modern people doing some of the things

254
00:13:08,681 --> 00:13:13,060
that these people did, I think they were, in a lot of cases,

255
00:13:13,160 --> 00:13:19,120
just fearless in going out in these small boats and storms

256
00:13:19,220 --> 00:13:20,600
that had wrecked much larger ships

257
00:13:20,700 --> 00:13:22,500
and trying to save somebody else.

258
00:13:22,600 --> 00:13:27,840
And it was all for very little pay, and they had no benefits.

259
00:13:27,940 --> 00:13:30,900
During the early years of the life-saving service,

260
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:33,640
a man named John Lyle came up with a better device

261
00:13:33,740 --> 00:13:35,380
for sending a line out to the boats.

262
00:13:38,680 --> 00:13:41,980
The Lyle gun fired a projectile that took a line out

263
00:13:42,080 --> 00:13:43,620
to the sinking vessel.

264
00:13:43,720 --> 00:13:47,920
The line could reach out 600 yards.

265
00:13:48,020 --> 00:13:50,860
Actually, the gun that Lyle invented,

266
00:13:50,960 --> 00:13:55,460
they were still making rescues basically 60 years later

267
00:13:55,560 --> 00:13:56,840
with this gun.

268
00:13:56,940 --> 00:13:58,960
It was still the most efficient way to get a line out

269
00:13:59,060 --> 00:14:00,760
to a vessel.

270
00:14:00,860 --> 00:14:03,480
The rescue personnel from the lifesaving service

271
00:14:03,580 --> 00:14:06,280
would patrol the beach looking for flares

272
00:14:06,281 --> 00:14:08,780
from ships in distress.

273
00:14:08,880 --> 00:14:10,820
When they spot a wreck, of course,

274
00:14:10,920 --> 00:14:13,320
they have to run back to the station, which again is a mile

275
00:14:13,420 --> 00:14:15,020
or two back potentially.

276
00:14:15,120 --> 00:14:17,560
They have to make a decision then

277
00:14:17,660 --> 00:14:22,460
whether to use the Lyle gun to try to make the rescue from shore

278
00:14:22,560 --> 00:14:23,760
or to use some kind of a boat.

279
00:14:23,860 --> 00:14:27,560
So the East Coast stations had what was called a surf boat,

280
00:14:27,660 --> 00:14:30,680
and that would be taken to the wreck on a wagon.

281
00:14:30,780 --> 00:14:34,080
And either the men pulled it by themselves, by lines,

282
00:14:34,081 --> 00:14:36,720
or they had horses that took it to the beach

283
00:14:36,820 --> 00:14:39,460
and to the side of the wreck.

284
00:14:39,560 --> 00:14:42,520
The rescuers would physically go to the failing vessel,

285
00:14:42,620 --> 00:14:44,720
or they would send up a line and attach

286
00:14:44,820 --> 00:14:47,860
either a life car, which was heavy but could carry several

287
00:14:47,960 --> 00:14:50,760
people at once, or a lighter breeches buoy

288
00:14:50,860 --> 00:14:52,260
to bring the victims ashore.

289
00:14:55,260 --> 00:14:57,540
And they have the breeches buoy, which is the way

290
00:14:57,640 --> 00:15:01,580
that the people got from the ship to the shore, which

291
00:15:01,680 --> 00:15:03,280
is basically looks like a life preserver

292
00:15:03,281 --> 00:15:06,280
with cut off pants in it, where you actually sit into this

293
00:15:06,380 --> 00:15:09,980
into this device and are carried from the ship to the shore.

294
00:15:10,080 --> 00:15:14,360
In 1915, Sumner Kimball merged the lifesaving service

295
00:15:14,460 --> 00:15:18,000
with the revenue cutter service and formed the United States

296
00:15:18,100 --> 00:15:19,360
Coast Guard.

297
00:15:19,460 --> 00:15:22,860
It seemed a natural combination, since both were patrolling

298
00:15:22,960 --> 00:15:25,760
the coastline, and Kimball wanted to get benefits

299
00:15:25,860 --> 00:15:28,700
for the hardworking men in his bureau.

300
00:15:28,800 --> 00:15:32,100
So they were literally serving until they were almost dead.

301
00:15:32,101 --> 00:15:35,600
The Coast Guard's most famous lifesaver, Joshua James,

302
00:15:35,700 --> 00:15:37,180
served into his late 60s.

303
00:15:37,280 --> 00:15:38,940
And he literally, at a beach drill,

304
00:15:39,040 --> 00:15:42,240
just dropped over dead on the beach.

305
00:15:42,340 --> 00:15:45,520
In the last 75 years, the Coast Guard

306
00:15:45,620 --> 00:15:47,620
has also been in the lead in developing

307
00:15:47,720 --> 00:15:51,160
more sophisticated communication systems for sending

308
00:15:51,260 --> 00:15:54,560
and receiving distress signals.

309
00:15:54,660 --> 00:15:57,420
The sinking of the Titanic provided the impetus

310
00:15:57,520 --> 00:16:00,860
for developing a better means of signaling at sea.

311
00:16:00,861 --> 00:16:03,360
Besides Morse code, the crew had no means

312
00:16:03,460 --> 00:16:08,440
to communicate with vessels far beyond the line of sight.

313
00:16:08,540 --> 00:16:11,640
Because of the Titanic disaster, the Coast Guard

314
00:16:11,740 --> 00:16:15,740
implemented the International Ice Patrol that still tracks

315
00:16:15,840 --> 00:16:18,640
icebergs as they flow from the North Atlantic

316
00:16:18,740 --> 00:16:19,840
into the shipping lanes.

317
00:16:22,820 --> 00:16:26,220
After the 1915 merger with the Revenue Cutter Service,

318
00:16:26,320 --> 00:16:28,380
the rescue technologies of the Coast Guard

319
00:16:28,381 --> 00:16:31,920
began to improve significantly.

320
00:16:32,020 --> 00:16:33,860
During World War II, the Coast Guard

321
00:16:33,960 --> 00:16:36,820
was given the task of developing the helicopter

322
00:16:36,920 --> 00:16:40,160
as a reconnaissance tool for the military.

323
00:16:40,260 --> 00:16:42,320
They were looking at it to spot submarines,

324
00:16:42,420 --> 00:16:44,260
but there were a lot of Coast Guard officers,

325
00:16:44,360 --> 00:16:48,160
one in particular, Frank Erickson, who saw the rescue

326
00:16:48,260 --> 00:16:49,600
potentials of the helicopter.

327
00:16:49,700 --> 00:16:51,540
And he had pushed this all along.

328
00:16:51,640 --> 00:16:55,100
And the helicopter, in many ways,

329
00:16:55,101 --> 00:16:58,600
is the next great change for the life-saving service

330
00:16:58,700 --> 00:17:01,940
or the life-saving capabilities of the Coast Guard.

331
00:17:02,040 --> 00:17:07,480
The helicopter proved to be a vital tool in March of 1990.

332
00:17:07,580 --> 00:17:10,920
Coast Guard rescued six crew members of the Alaskan monarch

333
00:17:11,020 --> 00:17:13,520
from the icy Bering Sea.

334
00:17:13,620 --> 00:17:16,080
The Coast Guard's boat couldn't get through the ice,

335
00:17:16,180 --> 00:17:17,860
but after the mayday was transmitted,

336
00:17:17,960 --> 00:17:22,720
the helicopter arrived on the scene within minutes.

337
00:17:22,721 --> 00:17:35,640
Certainly the technology of aircraft has improved significantly since the 50s, and our aircraft of today, our Jayhawk helicopters and our Dauphine H-65 helicopters,

338
00:17:35,641 --> 00:17:41,420
are really what we would like to consider state-of-the-art when it comes to a search-and-rescue tool.

339
00:17:41,421 --> 00:17:45,360
Throughout its 200-year history,

340
00:17:45,460 --> 00:17:48,220
the Coast Guard has been committed to developing boats

341
00:17:48,320 --> 00:17:53,360
and cutters that perform safely and efficiently in high winds

342
00:17:53,460 --> 00:17:54,760
and rough sea conditions.

343
00:17:57,260 --> 00:17:59,980
The first self-righting, self-bailing vessel

344
00:18:00,080 --> 00:18:02,780
dates back to 19th century England.

345
00:18:05,820 --> 00:18:09,980
The most recent version of the motor lifeboat is 47 feet

346
00:18:09,981 --> 00:18:13,480
and can right itself in five seconds in heavy seas.

347
00:18:20,220 --> 00:18:22,720
It is engineered with special ballast

348
00:18:22,820 --> 00:18:25,700
and an iron keel that work with the force of gravity

349
00:18:25,800 --> 00:18:28,440
to turn the boat right side up.

350
00:18:28,540 --> 00:18:32,040
An onboard engine automatically pumps any excess water

351
00:18:32,140 --> 00:18:33,040
out of the boat.

352
00:18:33,041 --> 00:18:40,040
the boat.

353
00:18:40,041 --> 00:18:45,040
The 47-foot motor lifeboat is the newest addition to the Coast Guard small boat fleet.

354
00:18:45,041 --> 00:18:49,780
It is on the leading edge of search and rescue technology.

355
00:18:49,781 --> 00:18:57,420
It gives us the capability to go out in very bad seas and bring both the folks in distress

356
00:18:57,421 --> 00:19:02,700
and our own crew back in a safe manner.

357
00:19:02,701 --> 00:19:06,700
It's large enough that it can handle very severe conditions,

358
00:19:06,800 --> 00:19:09,880
but it also has the strength and the technology

359
00:19:09,980 --> 00:19:13,780
to really do a very good job of getting through very difficult

360
00:19:13,880 --> 00:19:16,280
conditions and doing a rescue mission.

361
00:19:19,780 --> 00:19:22,180
In the Coast Guard Search and Rescue Unit,

362
00:19:22,280 --> 00:19:25,160
communication is the name of the game.

363
00:19:25,260 --> 00:19:28,300
The faster they make contact with the vessel in distress,

364
00:19:28,400 --> 00:19:31,900
the quicker they can pinpoint their location.

365
00:19:31,901 --> 00:19:38,620
The Coast Guard has a very extensive communication system, both medium frequency, high frequency,

366
00:19:38,621 --> 00:19:47,280
and also VHF FM radios, which is the most common means that are used for mariners to communicate

367
00:19:47,281 --> 00:19:48,660
with the Coast Guard.

368
00:19:48,661 --> 00:19:55,000
If we have a good location on an individual, if we know where they are through radio or

369
00:19:55,001 --> 00:20:01,300
whatever communications means that we have, then it essentially eliminates the search

370
00:20:01,301 --> 00:20:03,100
from search and rescue.

371
00:20:03,200 --> 00:20:05,940
The Coast Guard is developing new technologies

372
00:20:06,040 --> 00:20:08,900
that will enhance the world of search and rescue

373
00:20:09,000 --> 00:20:12,140
in the future.

374
00:20:12,240 --> 00:20:15,540
We have a vision 2020, which we're looking into the early

375
00:20:15,640 --> 00:20:17,760
21st century for development.

376
00:20:17,860 --> 00:20:21,260
And I mean, you're talking about some things that right now

377
00:20:21,360 --> 00:20:23,460
don't look realistic.

378
00:20:23,560 --> 00:20:25,900
Vacuum cleaners from aircraft to pick people up.

379
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:27,460
It sounds ridiculous.

380
00:20:27,461 --> 00:20:32,740
But obviously in the 1800s, a piece of metal with a rotary

381
00:20:32,840 --> 00:20:36,240
wing on top flapping in the sky, picking people up out of the

382
00:20:36,340 --> 00:20:37,640
water seemed ridiculous too.

383
00:20:42,900 --> 00:20:44,640
We see it all too often.

384
00:20:44,740 --> 00:20:49,080
Crumpled, mangled metal, a vehicle beyond recognition.

385
00:20:49,180 --> 00:20:51,180
For rescue workers, the main goal

386
00:20:51,280 --> 00:20:53,860
is to extract the driver and any passengers

387
00:20:53,960 --> 00:20:56,460
from the crushed remains of the automobile

388
00:20:56,461 --> 00:20:59,500
and get the medical care as soon as possible.

389
00:20:59,600 --> 00:21:03,000
They call it heavy entrapment.

390
00:21:03,100 --> 00:21:04,900
The first thing a rescuer is taught

391
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:06,340
when he arrived at the accident scene

392
00:21:06,440 --> 00:21:11,000
is try the door handle, because it just might open.

393
00:21:11,100 --> 00:21:13,820
And if it does, you're off to a good start.

394
00:21:13,920 --> 00:21:18,580
And basically, if you need to bend metal, move metal,

395
00:21:18,680 --> 00:21:20,720
that's heavy entrapment.

396
00:21:20,820 --> 00:21:23,260
Up until the late 60s, rescuers

397
00:21:23,360 --> 00:21:25,920
had a difficult time extricating victims

398
00:21:25,921 --> 00:21:29,660
from a vehicle that had been crushed in an accident.

399
00:21:29,760 --> 00:21:31,820
They would use a Porter Power, which

400
00:21:31,920 --> 00:21:35,560
is a manually pumped small tool that

401
00:21:35,660 --> 00:21:39,360
is used in the sheet metal business.

402
00:21:39,460 --> 00:21:44,100
They also used a product called a pry bar.

403
00:21:44,200 --> 00:21:46,340
They would use brute force.

404
00:21:46,440 --> 00:21:48,580
They would use hacksaws.

405
00:21:48,680 --> 00:21:51,740
Over the last 30 years, a powerful tool

406
00:21:51,840 --> 00:21:55,920
has evolved that can open up an automobile in mere minutes.

407
00:21:55,921 --> 00:21:58,120
Because of its incredible strength,

408
00:21:58,220 --> 00:22:00,660
they call it the Jaws of Life.

409
00:22:00,760 --> 00:22:02,520
And with the development of this tool

410
00:22:02,620 --> 00:22:05,560
came a new way of looking at the accident scene.

411
00:22:05,660 --> 00:22:08,560
A heavy entrapment is basically when the metal is folded up

412
00:22:08,660 --> 00:22:11,360
around a person, and you have to redirect it

413
00:22:11,460 --> 00:22:13,480
to get it away from them.

414
00:22:13,580 --> 00:22:15,880
We don't take the person out of the vehicle.

415
00:22:15,980 --> 00:22:18,380
We take the vehicle from around the person.

416
00:22:18,480 --> 00:22:20,720
My philosophy is a go-kart philosophy.

417
00:22:20,820 --> 00:22:22,880
If I can make that car look like a go-kart,

418
00:22:22,980 --> 00:22:25,920
there's nothing around that person to handle it.

419
00:22:25,921 --> 00:22:28,760
removal and packaging and stabilizing,

420
00:22:28,860 --> 00:22:32,220
the less I have to manipulate that person to package them,

421
00:22:32,320 --> 00:22:36,660
the less chance of causing more aggravation to an injury.

422
00:22:36,760 --> 00:22:38,860
The revolutionary tool that could

423
00:22:38,960 --> 00:22:42,100
cut and spread metal quickly was first developed

424
00:22:42,200 --> 00:22:45,200
by George Hurst in the 1960s.

425
00:22:45,300 --> 00:22:48,740
Its first application was in the world of professional car

426
00:22:48,840 --> 00:22:50,980
racing.

427
00:22:51,080 --> 00:22:54,980
The tool was originally called the Hurst Power Rescue Tool.

428
00:22:54,981 --> 00:23:01,980
George Hurst, who was the inventor, was the owner of a company that made racing equipment,

429
00:23:01,981 --> 00:23:11,300
engine conversion kits, and that sort of thing for aftermarket racing, and also for IndyCar's

430
00:23:11,301 --> 00:23:14,740
Daytona type racing circle track cars.

431
00:23:14,741 --> 00:23:19,460
And George had this vision of freeing trap drivers that would get entangled in the roll

432
00:23:19,461 --> 00:23:24,220
cage of an automobile and give them a better chance of survival.

433
00:23:24,221 --> 00:23:26,660
So he decided to take his ingenuity that he used

434
00:23:26,760 --> 00:23:29,960
in other things and apply them to racing.

435
00:23:30,060 --> 00:23:31,960
In turn two, we have a report of a problem.

436
00:23:32,060 --> 00:23:33,360
The jaws of life.

437
00:23:33,460 --> 00:23:34,960
That's what that signal indicates.

438
00:23:35,060 --> 00:23:40,060
They're going to want those jaws to try and pry the car out.

439
00:23:40,160 --> 00:23:43,080
By the late 1960s, Hurst's invention

440
00:23:43,180 --> 00:23:46,380
became an indispensable part of track safety.

441
00:23:46,480 --> 00:23:48,720
And in the early 1970s, he decided

442
00:23:48,820 --> 00:23:51,420
to bring the technology off the racetrack

443
00:23:51,520 --> 00:23:53,280
and onto the streets.

444
00:23:53,281 --> 00:23:55,820
He went about making a smaller portable version that

445
00:23:55,920 --> 00:23:58,860
could be used in everyday traffic accidents.

446
00:23:58,960 --> 00:24:00,480
It was at this time that the tool

447
00:24:00,580 --> 00:24:04,560
got the name that is its trademark today.

448
00:24:04,660 --> 00:24:07,160
It was called the Hurst Power Rescue Tool.

449
00:24:07,260 --> 00:24:09,700
And during one of the demonstrations,

450
00:24:09,800 --> 00:24:12,560
someone had said, wow, this tool saves people

451
00:24:12,660 --> 00:24:14,460
from the jaws of death.

452
00:24:14,560 --> 00:24:18,180
And subsequently, we started calling it the Jaws of Life.

453
00:24:18,280 --> 00:24:21,240
And that name stuck, and it sticks to this day.

454
00:24:21,241 --> 00:24:24,040
The release of the Jaws of Life for public use

455
00:24:24,140 --> 00:24:27,440
came as a welcome and much needed addition for rescue teams

456
00:24:27,540 --> 00:24:30,580
all over the country.

457
00:24:30,680 --> 00:24:35,580
Back in the 60s, when I began, there were no Jaws of Life.

458
00:24:35,680 --> 00:24:40,100
We used large crowbars to extricate people from vehicles.

459
00:24:40,200 --> 00:24:42,200
And many of them didn't get extricated.

460
00:24:42,300 --> 00:24:45,140
They were in there, and they died in there.

461
00:24:45,240 --> 00:24:47,640
With the advent of the Jaws of Life,

462
00:24:47,740 --> 00:24:50,640
you can peel a car apart in a matter of a few minutes.

463
00:24:50,641 --> 00:24:53,100
They can just pop the tops off vehicles

464
00:24:53,200 --> 00:24:55,640
and just split a car open, remove doors,

465
00:24:55,740 --> 00:24:58,540
and right through steel.

466
00:24:58,640 --> 00:25:01,320
At its widest, the arms of the jaws of life

467
00:25:01,420 --> 00:25:03,020
can spread up to 40 inches.

468
00:25:07,320 --> 00:25:09,460
The pressure generated between the arms

469
00:25:09,560 --> 00:25:13,400
can produce 35,000 pounds of force.

470
00:25:13,500 --> 00:25:16,000
This force is produced by a gasoline engine

471
00:25:16,100 --> 00:25:19,700
with a reciprocating pump.

472
00:25:19,701 --> 00:25:30,700
We developed a pump that had two stages, one high flow, low pressure, which would run from zero to 600 psi line pressure.

473
00:25:30,880 --> 00:25:43,720
And then once it met resistance, it would shift into a low flow, high pressure that would go from 600 psi up to 5,000 psi at a half gallon a minute.

474
00:25:44,140 --> 00:25:48,740
The tool captured the imagination of rescue workers around the country.

475
00:25:48,741 --> 00:25:53,740
But the cost from $6,000 to $12,000 was sometimes prohibitive.

476
00:25:53,741 --> 00:26:00,480
Oftentimes the fire department, rescue squad, whatever agency it might be that's interested

477
00:26:00,481 --> 00:26:03,660
in purchasing this product, didn't have the money.

478
00:26:03,661 --> 00:26:07,340
So they would do what they normally do, and that's go into fundraising.

479
00:26:07,341 --> 00:26:12,420
That can vary anywhere from standing on a street corner with boots or helmets expecting

480
00:26:12,421 --> 00:26:16,860
to deposit some coins or something at a traffic signal.

481
00:26:16,960 --> 00:26:21,700
Down through pancake dinners, ham suppers, spaghetti dinners,

482
00:26:21,800 --> 00:26:25,560
whatever it might be, to target reaching a goal.

483
00:26:29,640 --> 00:26:31,500
Over the last 27 years,

484
00:26:31,600 --> 00:26:34,640
Hearst has been dedicated to refining the jaws of life

485
00:26:34,740 --> 00:26:37,780
to fit more of the needs of the rescue community.

486
00:26:37,880 --> 00:26:41,520
At their manufacturing facility in Shelby, North Carolina,

487
00:26:41,521 --> 00:26:44,520
They research and develop new products every year

488
00:26:44,620 --> 00:26:48,620
and test new techniques for quicker, safer extrication.

489
00:26:48,720 --> 00:26:50,860
The tool we use to remove the windshield today

490
00:26:50,960 --> 00:26:53,060
is called the slice of life.

491
00:26:53,160 --> 00:26:57,160
It gives us better control of glass fragging

492
00:26:57,260 --> 00:26:59,280
through the passenger compartment.

493
00:26:59,380 --> 00:27:02,980
Years ago, we used to use an ax.

494
00:27:03,080 --> 00:27:05,580
We've come out with a product we call the Warthog

495
00:27:05,680 --> 00:27:09,320
that allows us to stabilize the car so it stays put.

496
00:27:09,321 --> 00:27:16,000
We have lifting pneumatic bags that can lift things off of people or move things out of the way that need to be moved

497
00:27:18,260 --> 00:27:24,700
We recently introduced the pyrotechnic cutter which uses a cartridge and is totally portable

498
00:27:25,940 --> 00:27:33,180
Every year rescue teams save thousands of lives armed with the power of the jaws of life

499
00:27:39,320 --> 00:27:45,600
Most firefighters, rescue people, sometime in their life has been touched by a fire,

500
00:27:45,601 --> 00:27:51,440
whether a house or a family relative had a house burned down, or the family member or

501
00:27:51,441 --> 00:27:56,360
a good friend got caught in a car accident and badly injured.

502
00:27:56,361 --> 00:27:58,820
Those are the things that motivate a lot of people.

503
00:27:58,821 --> 00:28:03,080
They want to be useful and helpful, and they don't want to see that happen to anybody else.

504
00:28:09,320 --> 00:28:16,320
The powerful force of nature strikes without warning.

505
00:28:22,860 --> 00:28:26,540
An avalanche can be triggered by the slightest pressure

506
00:28:26,541 --> 00:28:29,640
and can cause the most devastating destruction

507
00:28:29,641 --> 00:28:33,480
in its incredible wake.

508
00:28:33,481 --> 00:28:35,440
When the slab avalanche releases,

509
00:28:35,441 --> 00:28:37,920
they accelerate almost instantly.

510
00:28:37,921 --> 00:28:39,720
They're racing down the mountainside.

511
00:28:39,820 --> 00:28:44,020
And a moderate-sized avalanche might be one that falls 800

512
00:28:44,120 --> 00:28:45,760
to 1,000 vertical feet.

513
00:28:45,860 --> 00:28:50,560
It might be 300 feet across and perhaps three feet deep.

514
00:28:50,660 --> 00:28:52,300
And that's going to fall down the mountainside

515
00:28:52,400 --> 00:28:56,240
at speeds of 60, 70, 80, 90 miles an hour.

516
00:28:56,340 --> 00:29:00,280
Avalanche rescue technology has been around since the beginning

517
00:29:00,380 --> 00:29:01,380
of recorded history.

518
00:29:01,381 --> 00:29:12,580
We have to go back over 2,000 years ago to the Greek geographer Strabo, who traveled

519
00:29:12,581 --> 00:29:16,200
around the Caucasus Mountains near the Black Sea.

520
00:29:16,201 --> 00:29:22,620
And he writes of the shepherds carrying staffs not just for their sheep, but if they were

521
00:29:22,621 --> 00:29:26,060
buried in an avalanche, they could stick the staff up through the snow.

522
00:29:26,061 --> 00:29:30,980
And I think it actually got kind of turned around a little bit because when you're buried

523
00:29:30,981 --> 00:29:32,220
you can't move.

524
00:29:32,320 --> 00:29:34,580
You can't even push anything through the surface.

525
00:29:34,680 --> 00:29:37,680
And I think these staffs were used to help find people

526
00:29:37,780 --> 00:29:41,620
that were buried underneath the snow.

527
00:29:41,720 --> 00:29:44,620
The Roman poet Silius Italicus wrote

528
00:29:44,720 --> 00:29:49,140
about Hannibal in 218 BC, who lost thousands of men

529
00:29:49,240 --> 00:29:52,560
and scores of elephants in avalanches

530
00:29:52,660 --> 00:29:53,940
while crossing the Alps.

531
00:29:57,240 --> 00:30:00,380
In approximately 1050 AD, St. Bernard

532
00:30:00,381 --> 00:30:04,280
and his monks began patrolling the Alps searching for travelers

533
00:30:04,380 --> 00:30:08,620
who were caught in the onslaught of snow.

534
00:30:08,720 --> 00:30:11,820
The monks deployed large dogs known as berry dogs

535
00:30:11,920 --> 00:30:14,620
to assist them in finding those travelers who had succumbed

536
00:30:14,720 --> 00:30:17,600
to the White Death.

537
00:30:17,700 --> 00:30:21,140
These gentle giants had an excellent sense of direction

538
00:30:21,240 --> 00:30:25,640
and could maneuver easily through the thick snow.

539
00:30:25,740 --> 00:30:29,380
Napoleon wrote that when he crossed the Alps in 1800,

540
00:30:29,381 --> 00:30:32,820
he didn't lose any of his 250,000 men,

541
00:30:32,920 --> 00:30:34,820
thanks to the monks and their dogs.

542
00:30:37,620 --> 00:30:40,360
The image of the St. Bernard with the brandy keg

543
00:30:40,460 --> 00:30:43,500
reached mythic proportions, even though the dogs

544
00:30:43,600 --> 00:30:45,900
never carried alcohol.

545
00:30:46,000 --> 00:30:48,960
The barrels were used to transport milk to the monastery

546
00:30:49,060 --> 00:30:49,940
from the dairy.

547
00:30:52,140 --> 00:30:55,880
For centuries, avalanches continued to plague the Alps

548
00:30:55,980 --> 00:30:59,380
and even played a decisive role in the First World War.

549
00:30:59,381 --> 00:31:07,740
Avalanches were really a powerful weapon of war in World War I, killing tens of thousands

550
00:31:07,741 --> 00:31:08,740
of soldiers.

551
00:31:08,741 --> 00:31:19,100
And depending which historian we look at, we can say that as many as 40,000 to 60,000

552
00:31:19,101 --> 00:31:24,000
soldiers were killed by avalanches during the three winters of World War I.

553
00:31:24,001 --> 00:31:29,400
In fact, in December of 1916, 6,000 troops,

554
00:31:29,500 --> 00:31:33,780
Austrian and Italian soldiers, were killed by avalanches

555
00:31:33,880 --> 00:31:36,580
in a 48-hour period.

556
00:31:36,680 --> 00:31:39,520
After World War II, more people went to the mountains

557
00:31:39,620 --> 00:31:43,320
for recreation and increased the need for rescue teams

558
00:31:43,420 --> 00:31:45,180
and rescue equipment.

559
00:31:45,280 --> 00:31:47,420
Experienced climbers would volunteer

560
00:31:47,520 --> 00:31:50,200
to go and search for those buried under the snow,

561
00:31:50,300 --> 00:31:54,000
using the rudimentary tools that mountaineers had at the time.

562
00:31:54,001 --> 00:32:00,000
A lot of the equipment in Mountain Rescue really hasn't changed that much in the last 50 years.

563
00:32:00,740 --> 00:32:03,940
I like to say that really it's just the colors of the equipment are brighter.

564
00:32:04,740 --> 00:32:09,900
But going back 50 years ago, the basic tools of the Mountain Rescue were rope,

565
00:32:10,240 --> 00:32:15,760
some basic climbing equipment, and a backpack with a little extra clothing and food.

566
00:32:16,420 --> 00:32:19,980
And in essence, that's still a lot of what the equipment is that's used today.

567
00:32:20,420 --> 00:32:23,740
Nowadays, we have equipment made from titanium, very lightweight.

568
00:32:23,741 --> 00:32:28,980
In the early days, everything was made of steel and very heavy.

569
00:32:29,080 --> 00:32:30,880
The ropes were very heavy.

570
00:32:30,980 --> 00:32:32,580
Nowadays, everything's much more lightweight.

571
00:32:32,680 --> 00:32:34,580
And that's an important factor when

572
00:32:34,680 --> 00:32:36,760
you're carrying this equipment two and three miles into the

573
00:32:36,860 --> 00:32:39,920
field to the site of the rescue.

574
00:32:40,020 --> 00:32:42,920
One constant in the avalanche rescue world

575
00:32:43,020 --> 00:32:46,300
is the use of dogs to pick up the scent of those victims who

576
00:32:46,400 --> 00:32:50,160
are buried in deep, packed snow.

577
00:32:50,260 --> 00:32:53,200
A dog's nose is hundreds of times more sensitive

578
00:32:53,201 --> 00:32:56,000
than the humans.

579
00:32:56,100 --> 00:32:59,300
If you've got an avalanche debris field that's 100 by 100

580
00:32:59,400 --> 00:33:03,440
meters, and you take 20 rescuers into that area,

581
00:33:03,540 --> 00:33:05,440
it can take four to six hours for them

582
00:33:05,540 --> 00:33:08,140
to search that area thoroughly.

583
00:33:08,240 --> 00:33:10,820
Whereas a well-trained avalanche dog

584
00:33:10,920 --> 00:33:13,260
can search the same area in 25 minutes.

585
00:33:21,300 --> 00:33:22,260
Dogs are pretty amazing.

586
00:33:22,261 --> 00:33:25,300
get on a debris field where humans can't see anything.

587
00:33:25,400 --> 00:33:27,500
A dog will do a search pattern like Angel

588
00:33:27,600 --> 00:33:29,700
has done this zigzag search pattern.

589
00:33:29,800 --> 00:33:32,100
If we don't have any winds, and she'll locate the person,

590
00:33:32,200 --> 00:33:34,940
or she'll work downwind, and the human scent

591
00:33:35,040 --> 00:33:37,300
is emanating from the snowpack, and she

592
00:33:37,400 --> 00:33:40,420
can identify the person then.

593
00:33:40,520 --> 00:33:43,020
While dogs are extremely useful,

594
00:33:43,120 --> 00:33:45,880
a new electronic transceiver has been developed

595
00:33:45,980 --> 00:33:48,820
in the last 10 years that has revolutionized

596
00:33:48,920 --> 00:33:51,760
the ability of mountain rescuers to locate a person

597
00:33:51,761 --> 00:33:53,960
buried in the snow.

598
00:33:54,060 --> 00:33:56,060
The avalanche transceiver is certainly

599
00:33:56,160 --> 00:33:59,200
one of the most important technological breakthroughs

600
00:33:59,300 --> 00:34:01,800
of this century in terms of backcountry users.

601
00:34:01,900 --> 00:34:03,700
The transceiver is fairly simple.

602
00:34:03,800 --> 00:34:05,900
It's about the size of a Sony Walkman.

603
00:34:06,000 --> 00:34:06,900
I would have one.

604
00:34:07,000 --> 00:34:09,020
My skiing partner would have one.

605
00:34:09,120 --> 00:34:11,720
And if one of us were buried in an avalanche,

606
00:34:11,820 --> 00:34:13,520
the other would take their transceiver

607
00:34:13,620 --> 00:34:15,680
and attach to it a tiny little earpiece

608
00:34:15,780 --> 00:34:17,520
that we would put in the ear.

609
00:34:17,620 --> 00:34:20,960
And by changing the orientation of our beacon,

610
00:34:20,961 --> 00:34:25,760
we can virtually find somebody in an avalanche debris field

611
00:34:25,860 --> 00:34:29,860
that we can't see a single trace of with the naked eye.

612
00:34:29,960 --> 00:34:34,000
Rescuers also use global positioning system units

613
00:34:34,100 --> 00:34:38,540
that tell them their location at all times.

614
00:34:38,640 --> 00:34:41,940
15 years ago, when I started working in search and rescue,

615
00:34:42,040 --> 00:34:45,680
we had to be really, really strong in our map and compass

616
00:34:45,780 --> 00:34:48,580
skills so that we could identify where we were virtually

617
00:34:48,581 --> 00:34:51,480
virtually any time of the day or night.

618
00:34:51,580 --> 00:34:55,660
And nowadays, one of the greatest and most significant

619
00:34:55,760 --> 00:34:59,860
improvements to what we do is the GPS unit, Global Positioning

620
00:34:59,960 --> 00:35:03,900
System unit, which works by receiving signals

621
00:35:04,000 --> 00:35:07,160
from a multitude of satellites flying overhead

622
00:35:07,260 --> 00:35:10,600
and tells me where I am.

623
00:35:10,700 --> 00:35:14,400
Forecasters analyze terrain, snowpack conditions,

624
00:35:14,500 --> 00:35:18,580
and weather patterns to predict potential avalanche dangers.

625
00:35:18,581 --> 00:35:20,920
And while rescue methods have improved,

626
00:35:20,921 --> 00:35:24,180
sometimes the best weapon is prevention.

627
00:35:24,181 --> 00:35:25,620
Highway departments and ski areas

628
00:35:25,621 --> 00:35:28,420
use explosives to trigger avalanches,

629
00:35:28,421 --> 00:35:31,920
and they deliver the explosives a couple of different ways.

630
00:35:31,921 --> 00:35:36,240
They can use artillery weapons that are military surplus.

631
00:35:36,241 --> 00:35:39,660
We also use gas-propelled type cannons

632
00:35:39,661 --> 00:35:43,640
that will launch an explosive charge up onto a mountainside.

633
00:35:43,641 --> 00:35:46,480
Or the third way we do it is with hand charges,

634
00:35:46,481 --> 00:35:50,720
where ski patrollers will toss the explosive charges out

635
00:35:50,820 --> 00:35:54,480
onto the avalanche-prone slope to trigger the avalanche.

636
00:36:04,200 --> 00:36:06,300
With more people visiting the back country

637
00:36:06,400 --> 00:36:08,140
and mountains for skiing, hiking,

638
00:36:08,240 --> 00:36:10,640
and other recreational sports, the experts

639
00:36:10,740 --> 00:36:14,140
feel that becoming educated about avalanche dangers

640
00:36:14,141 --> 00:36:18,040
is the best way to prevent a disaster.

641
00:36:18,140 --> 00:36:20,340
You can have all the gadgets in the world,

642
00:36:20,440 --> 00:36:22,780
unless you really know how to safely use them.

643
00:36:22,880 --> 00:36:26,280
And when you're taking unnecessary risk,

644
00:36:26,380 --> 00:36:28,980
you're really not a safe user of the back country.

645
00:36:29,080 --> 00:36:31,900
So we're really trying to promote education as well.

646
00:36:32,000 --> 00:36:34,720
And that's something that sort of transcends technology

647
00:36:34,820 --> 00:36:36,300
and generations and that sort of thing.

648
00:36:44,140 --> 00:36:47,640
In the 1980s, the world of search and rescue

649
00:36:47,740 --> 00:36:52,140
joined the space age through its use of satellite technology.

650
00:36:52,240 --> 00:36:54,080
The technology is established.

651
00:36:54,180 --> 00:36:58,580
The use of it is fairly new just since the 80s.

652
00:36:58,680 --> 00:37:00,320
We've always had the capabilities

653
00:37:00,420 --> 00:37:03,620
to transmit signals to a satellite and process them,

654
00:37:03,720 --> 00:37:09,040
but to turn around and use those signals and locate people.

655
00:37:14,140 --> 00:37:17,140
The COSPAS-SARSAT program is a cooperative effort

656
00:37:17,240 --> 00:37:20,540
of France, the United States, Canada, and Russia.

657
00:37:20,640 --> 00:37:23,040
It is a satellite tracking system that picks up

658
00:37:23,140 --> 00:37:27,420
and relays signals from distress beacons around the world.

659
00:37:27,520 --> 00:37:30,420
COSPAS is an acronym for the Russian satellites

660
00:37:30,520 --> 00:37:32,660
that carry the equipment.

661
00:37:32,760 --> 00:37:36,260
SARSAT stands for Search and Rescue Satellite Aided Tracking.

662
00:37:36,360 --> 00:37:38,060
And it's actually the name of the instrument

663
00:37:38,160 --> 00:37:40,760
that flies on the NOAA satellites.

664
00:37:40,860 --> 00:37:44,140
NOAA, the National Oceanic and Atmospheric

665
00:37:44,141 --> 00:37:48,140
has satellites that monitor weather conditions from space.

666
00:37:48,240 --> 00:37:52,080
The SARSAT instrument is a payload on these satellites.

667
00:37:52,180 --> 00:37:54,220
Russia joined the program to help protect

668
00:37:54,320 --> 00:37:57,260
her large fishing fleet.

669
00:37:57,360 --> 00:37:59,660
And plus, they had just finished the Apollo-Soyuz

670
00:37:59,760 --> 00:38:03,100
venture with NASA, and they were looking for other space

671
00:38:03,200 --> 00:38:05,800
programs that they could be cooperative in.

672
00:38:05,900 --> 00:38:08,800
And when they saw the search and rescue business,

673
00:38:08,900 --> 00:38:10,800
they decided that was a good one for them

674
00:38:10,900 --> 00:38:14,140
to get on board with as well.

675
00:38:14,141 --> 00:38:17,640
The concept is relatively simple.

676
00:38:17,740 --> 00:38:20,320
Boats and airplanes are equipped with electronic locating

677
00:38:20,420 --> 00:38:23,420
transmitters, or beacons.

678
00:38:23,520 --> 00:38:25,520
If the boat or plane is in danger,

679
00:38:25,620 --> 00:38:28,180
the transmitter is automatically activated

680
00:38:28,280 --> 00:38:30,720
and sends a signal to the satellite.

681
00:38:30,820 --> 00:38:32,760
The satellite processes the information

682
00:38:32,860 --> 00:38:36,660
and relays it to the nearest ground terminal.

683
00:38:36,760 --> 00:38:40,360
The ground station transmits to a mission control center.

684
00:38:40,460 --> 00:38:42,940
Mission control centers are in the same way

685
00:38:42,941 --> 00:38:46,280
Mission Control determines which rescue coordination center

686
00:38:46,380 --> 00:38:51,180
is the closest and passes on the location information.

687
00:38:51,280 --> 00:38:54,480
Coast Guard Command Center, Lieutenant Commander Robbins.

688
00:38:54,580 --> 00:38:57,880
The mission control centers are located in South America

689
00:38:57,980 --> 00:39:00,300
in countries like Chile, Brazil, Peru.

690
00:39:00,400 --> 00:39:04,400
They're in Europe and UK, in France, Spain,

691
00:39:04,500 --> 00:39:12,240
Russia has one, India, Australia, New Zealand, China, Japan.

692
00:39:12,241 --> 00:39:14,280
The NOAA polar orbiting satellites

693
00:39:14,380 --> 00:39:17,740
are at an altitude of about 458 miles

694
00:39:17,840 --> 00:39:21,980
and travel once around the Earth every 90 minutes.

695
00:39:22,080 --> 00:39:25,780
Because they are so fast, when they detect a distress signal,

696
00:39:25,880 --> 00:39:29,060
they use a physical phenomenon called the Doppler shift

697
00:39:29,160 --> 00:39:33,220
to determine its exact location.

698
00:39:33,320 --> 00:39:35,560
When they detect the signal, they

699
00:39:35,660 --> 00:39:37,700
don't actually detect it at the frequency

700
00:39:37,800 --> 00:39:41,200
that the beacon's transmitting, much like a train whistle.

701
00:39:41,201 --> 00:39:43,540
When you hear a train whistle, if it's stationary,

702
00:39:43,640 --> 00:39:45,200
it sounds a certain way.

703
00:39:45,300 --> 00:39:47,940
But if it's coming towards you, the pitch changes.

704
00:39:48,040 --> 00:39:50,640
And as it's going away from you, the pitch changes.

705
00:39:50,740 --> 00:39:51,940
That's the Doppler shift.

706
00:39:52,040 --> 00:39:57,040
And that's the principle that we use to locate signals.

707
00:39:57,140 --> 00:39:58,960
The SARSAT instruments are also

708
00:39:59,060 --> 00:40:03,260
aboard the GOES, or Geostationary Operational

709
00:40:03,360 --> 00:40:05,760
Environmental Satellites.

710
00:40:05,860 --> 00:40:09,360
These are almost 23,000 miles up in space

711
00:40:09,361 --> 00:40:11,600
and rotate with the Earth.

712
00:40:11,700 --> 00:40:13,660
They receive the location information

713
00:40:13,760 --> 00:40:17,600
directly from the beacons, as long as the beacons

714
00:40:17,700 --> 00:40:20,000
are equipped with a global positioning system.

715
00:40:22,540 --> 00:40:24,000
The only way they can get a location

716
00:40:24,100 --> 00:40:29,180
is if the beacon itself transmits its position in its signal.

717
00:40:29,280 --> 00:40:31,280
And that's possible with newer beacons.

718
00:40:31,380 --> 00:40:35,320
They have, most of them have something that's called a GPS

719
00:40:35,420 --> 00:40:38,320
receiver, which actually gets its position

720
00:40:38,321 --> 00:40:42,020
from another series of satellites, the GPS satellites,

721
00:40:42,120 --> 00:40:45,120
it'll calculate its position and then send it in the signal

722
00:40:45,220 --> 00:40:50,200
to our satellites so that we know right away whose beacon

723
00:40:50,300 --> 00:40:53,700
is being activated as well as where it's being activated.

724
00:40:53,800 --> 00:40:56,280
Today's new GPS beacons operate

725
00:40:56,380 --> 00:40:59,700
on a separate designated frequency.

726
00:40:59,800 --> 00:41:02,780
This diminishes the possibility of false alarms

727
00:41:02,880 --> 00:41:06,020
that had been caused by inadvertent signals from garage

728
00:41:06,021 --> 00:41:10,260
door openers and other electronic devices.

729
00:41:10,360 --> 00:41:14,660
Out of 100 signals, for example, about 97 of those 100

730
00:41:14,760 --> 00:41:17,460
would be non-distress alerts is what we call them.

731
00:41:17,560 --> 00:41:19,160
Three of them, though, are distressed.

732
00:41:19,260 --> 00:41:21,760
So we have to treat them all like they're real.

733
00:41:21,860 --> 00:41:23,980
We look at it as kind of like detectives, really.

734
00:41:24,080 --> 00:41:24,880
We have a signal.

735
00:41:24,980 --> 00:41:25,680
We have a mystery.

736
00:41:25,681 --> 00:41:26,780
We're trying to solve it.

737
00:41:26,880 --> 00:41:29,180
We hope it's not distressed, but we have to plan

738
00:41:29,280 --> 00:41:31,180
for the fact that it is.

739
00:41:31,280 --> 00:41:36,020
From the first save in 1982 until today,

740
00:41:36,021 --> 00:41:38,220
The program has proven successful.

741
00:41:41,420 --> 00:41:43,520
We've seen over 12,000 lives saved

742
00:41:43,620 --> 00:41:44,920
since its inception.

743
00:41:45,020 --> 00:41:48,260
In the United States, in the past three years,

744
00:41:48,360 --> 00:41:53,780
we've had over 1,200 lives saved using the SARSAT system.

745
00:41:53,880 --> 00:41:56,580
So the impact is tremendous.

746
00:41:56,680 --> 00:42:00,980
We are allowing our assets to launch on a known position,

747
00:42:01,080 --> 00:42:02,980
so we're not wasting the taxpayers' money

748
00:42:03,080 --> 00:42:04,380
by flying around searching.

749
00:42:06,020 --> 00:42:10,020
The beacons are mandated for large ships and aircraft,

750
00:42:10,120 --> 00:42:12,660
but any mariner or aviator can buy one

751
00:42:12,760 --> 00:42:15,760
for between $8 and $1,200.

752
00:42:15,860 --> 00:42:17,600
There's a lot of people out there

753
00:42:17,700 --> 00:42:20,360
that may not necessarily need one of these beacons,

754
00:42:20,460 --> 00:42:23,140
but if I were in trouble, I would want one.

755
00:42:23,240 --> 00:42:25,700
We're able to actually track signals that in the past

756
00:42:25,800 --> 00:42:27,640
we wouldn't have even known about.

757
00:42:27,740 --> 00:42:30,340
I feel it's been a significant increase in our rescue

758
00:42:30,440 --> 00:42:32,120
capability by having the system.

759
00:42:32,220 --> 00:42:34,380
It's also a great thing for the populace to know

760
00:42:34,381 --> 00:42:38,640
populace to know that with their emergency equipment, you know, that they

761
00:42:38,641 --> 00:42:42,660
need to have on board, there's dedicated equipment that it's only used for that

762
00:42:42,661 --> 00:42:47,400
purpose to listen for it. This program is the most rewarding program I've ever

763
00:42:47,401 --> 00:42:51,540
been involved in, in the fact that you're saving lives, you're saving money, you're

764
00:42:51,541 --> 00:42:57,000
saving property, and it's just a great humanitarian project. I've been involved

765
00:42:57,001 --> 00:43:00,900
with search and rescue. I got involved in it a little bit when I was in the

766
00:43:00,901 --> 00:43:02,400
United States Air Force.

767
00:43:02,500 --> 00:43:05,440
And this just turned me on even more.

768
00:43:05,540 --> 00:43:08,400
And I think it is the most rewarding program I've ever

769
00:43:08,500 --> 00:43:11,140
been affiliated with.

770
00:43:11,240 --> 00:43:15,780
From the unseen, unheard world of satellite systems

771
00:43:15,880 --> 00:43:20,020
to the familiar sights and sounds of sirens and tools

772
00:43:20,120 --> 00:43:23,520
like the jaws of life, rescue technology

773
00:43:23,620 --> 00:43:28,020
is saving our most precious asset, human lives.

